"comparison": "Both BAC and C emphasize the necessity of hedge effectiveness for maintaining hedge accounting. BAC explicitly states that it discontinues hedge accounting if a derivative is determined to be not highly effective, aligning with C's rule that if a relationship is not highly effective, it must be de-designated. A key difference is that C discloses that subsequent fair value changes for de-designated derivatives are recognized in 'Other revenue or Principal transactions', whereas BAC's disclosure for this specific topic focuses on the discontinuation criterion itself without detailing the subsequent P&L impact in the cited text.",
